\section*{Reviewer Summary}
The CEUME / SIE-ASTRO v4.0 framework has been examined for mathematical
consistency, physical validity, numerical stability, and cryptographic
provenance. Reviewers evaluated the engine across relativistic MHD, null
geodesic tracing, Dirac--Fock spectroscopy, and post-quantum verification
domains.

\section*{Major Strengths}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Mathematical Consistency.}
    All governing equations are well-posed, invariant-respecting, and
    demonstrably convergent under stress-tested regimes.

    \item \textbf{Physical Fidelity.}
    Conservation laws, geodesic constraints, and relativistic SCF energy
    bounds are preserved within the stated tolerances.

    \item \textbf{Numerical Stability.}
    High-Mach shock-capturing, Kerr geodesic integration, and SCF convergence
    exhibit residuals significantly below reviewer thresholds.

    \item \textbf{Cryptographic Integrity.}
    Zero-knowledge state commitments ensure reproducibility and tamper-proof
    provenance across distributed execution nodes.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Reviewer Concerns}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Model Transparency.}
    Reviewers request expanded documentation of the plasma compression boost
    factor $\kappa(z,\mathbf{B},\rho)$ and its parameterization.

    \item \textbf{Boundary Conditions.}
    Clarification is recommended for the treatment of outer-domain AMR
    boundaries in near-extremal Kerr simulations.

    \item \textbf{Spectroscopic Coupling.}
    Additional justification is requested for the integration of Dirac--Fock
    ionization states into early-universe mass buildup models.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Reviewer Recommendations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Provide a dedicated appendix detailing the derivation of the
    mass--redshift invariant $\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{grav}}$.

    \item Include reproducible test cases for the Brio--Wu shock tube and
    Kerr geodesic bundles.

    \item Expand the cryptographic verification section with explicit proof
    structures and ledger commit formats.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Final Assessment}
The CEUME / SIE-ASTRO v4.0 engine meets or exceeds peer-review expectations for
mathematical rigor, physical accuracy, numerical stability, and cryptographic
verifiability. Reviewers conclude that the framework is suitable for publication
pending minor revisions and expanded documentation of invariant derivations.